---
import DocLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import TechBadge from '../../../../components/TechBadge.astro';
import AttestreeDiagram from '../../../../components/diagrams/AttestreeDiagram.astro';
import docNavs from '../../../../data/doc-navs.en.json';
const nav = docNavs['attestree'];

const tech = ['.NET', 'winget', 'in-toto / SLSA', 'SBOM / CycloneDX', 'Sigstore', 'PostgreSQL', 'Docker', 'Astro'];

const projectSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
  "name": "Attestree",
  "applicationCategory": "DeveloperApplication",
  "operatingSystem": "Windows",
  "description": "Fleet deployment control plane for Windows that attests every winget package at ingest — sandboxed detonation, observed SBOM, and signed in-toto / SLSA provenance — then serves it from a fenced feed through ring-based rollout.",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Marc Borer",
    "url": "https://marc-borer.ch"
  },
  "programmingLanguage": ["C#", "TypeScript"],
  "keywords": "software supply chain, winget, attestation, SBOM, in-toto, SLSA, provenance, Windows fleet, ring deployment, private winget source"
});
---

<DocLayout title="Attestree — Attested winget for Windows Fleets" description="Fleet deployment control plane that attests every winget package at ingest — sandboxed detonation, observed SBOM, signed in-toto / SLSA provenance — and serves it from a fenced feed through ring-based rollout." ogImage="/og/attestree.png" sidebarTitle={nav.title} sidebarItems={nav.items}>
  <article class="case-study">
    <a href="/en/projects/" class="back-link">
      <span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Back to Projects
    </a>

    <header class="case-header" data-animate="slide-up">
      <h1 class="case-title">Attestree</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">Attested winget for Windows fleets — signed provenance at ingest</p>
      <div class="case-tech">
        {tech.map((t) => <TechBadge name={t} />)}
      </div>
      <p class="case-private">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="3" y="11" width="18" height="11" rx="2" ry="2"/>
          <path d="M7 11V7a5 5 0 0110 0v4"/>
        </svg>
        Private Repository
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="case-section" data-animate="slide-up">
      <h2>Problem</h2>
      <p>
        Most Windows shops install packages straight from the public source and discover problems
        only after a scanner runs against an endpoint days later. By then the artifact is already
        running across the fleet, SBOMs are an afterthought, attestations are missing, and the
        supply-chain story is "we trust Microsoft's CDN." Tanium, Defender for Cloud, Snyk, and
        their peers scan deployed binaries after the fact. Nobody inspects the catalog and
        distribution layer before deploy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" data-animate="slide-up">
      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>
        Attestree inverts the order: <em>winget install, but with a receipt.</em> Every package is
        attested at <strong>ingest</strong>, not after install. The installer's signature and
        publisher continuity are checked, the package is detonated in a sandbox, a CycloneDX SBOM
        is generated from what the install actually did, and policy-as-code decides. Admitted
        artifacts carry a signed in-toto statement with SLSA v1 provenance that an auditor verifies
        with a public key. Endpoints are then fenced to the operator's own attested winget feed and
        receive packages through groups and rings, with the agent re-verifying the exact approved
        hash at install.
      </p>
    </section>

    <AttestreeDiagram />

    <section class="case-section" data-animate="slide-up">
      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        The system has two surfaces. A control plane with an endpoint agent runs the ingest gate,
        attestation, and deployment. The public front door is the marketing site plus an open-core
        community edition.
      </p>

      <h3>Ingest Gate</h3>
      <p>
        Each package passes through fetch, installer-signature and publisher-certificate checks,
        sandboxed detonation, an observed CycloneDX 1.6 SBOM, and a policy-as-code decision. The
        result is signed as an in-toto statement (ECDSA P-256). winget is live, and Chocolatey is
        managed under the same control plane. MSI/MST transforms, driver rings, and further
        package managers are on the roadmap.
      </p>

      <h3>Fenced Feed, Groups × Rings</h3>
      <p>
        Endpoints install from the operator's own spec-compliant winget REST source, pinned to the
        approved SHA-256; a normal <code>winget install</code> never reaches the public repository.
        PC Groups decide <em>what</em> a device runs; rings (canary → pilot → broad → all) decide
        <em>which version</em>. Rollback goes to any prior version. A failing rollout produces a
        rollback proposal for the operator to confirm rather than acting on its own.
      </p>

      <h3>Unattended Updates with a Halt Queue</h3>
      <p>
        An opt-in pipeline ingests, detonates, and promotes new winget versions ring by ring. It
        halts for review when a publisher's signing certificate changes or an installer arrives
        unsigned, which is exactly the signal a hijacked release produces.
      </p>

      <h3>Control Plane Stack</h3>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>.NET</strong>: Native AOT installer shim and a self-updating endpoint agent rolled out through the same rings</li>
        <li><strong>Docker + PostgreSQL</strong>: server image with a Postgres sidecar; per-tenant scope as a first-class invariant</li>
        <li><strong>Open formats</strong>: in-toto, SLSA v1, CycloneDX 1.6 (Syft), with Sigstore supported but not required</li>
        <li><strong>Fleet CVE index</strong>: self-hosted, matching observed inventory against cvelistV5 and CISA KEV, with an offline bundle for air-gapped hosts</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" data-animate="slide-up">
      <h2>Key Technical Decisions</h2>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Attest at ingest, not scan after install</h3>
        <p>
          The structural wedge: Attestree owns the catalog/distribution layer and verifies before
          deploy. Without pre-deploy attestation, "we inventory it" is just a fancier
          <code>winget list</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>An observed SBOM, not a declared one</h3>
        <p>
          The SBOM is generated from what the installer actually did in the sandbox, not taken
          from the publisher's manifest. An ingest-time SBOM is an observation, and that is what
          makes it worth signing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Three enforcement points</h3>
        <p>
          For a Windows fleet the endpoint is the load-bearing enforcement point, so policy is not
          a single pipeline check. The ingest gate blocks before the catalog, reconciliation
          catches drift against declared state, and the agent re-verifies signature, hash, and
          policy at install.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Humans confirm the destructive step</h3>
        <p>
          Automation proposes and people approve. Rollback proposals are confirm-gated and off by
          default, and the update pipeline halts into a review queue instead of guessing when a
          publisher's signing identity changes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Open-core with an enforced license boundary</h3>
        <p>
          The installer shim and its IPC contract are Apache-2.0; the platform is closed-source. The
          license boundary is enforced mechanically in the build, so closed-source code cannot be
          relicensed by accident. The free community edition runs self-hosted on Docker Compose
          with no cloud dependency, ships as Sigstore-cosign signed images, and includes roles,
          TOTP two-factor, and operational runbooks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" data-animate="slide-up">
      <h2>Public Site</h2>
      <p>
        The marketing site at attestree.com is a separate, fully public surface: a static Astro
        build with Tailwind + MDX, deployed to Cloudflare Pages with a preview deploy per PR. The
        waitlist runs as a same-origin Cloudflare Pages Function backed by D1 in an EU jurisdiction
        with hardened Turnstile verification. Site copy is re-synced against every platform
        release, with an explicit shipped-vs-roadmap split, and unbacked claims are removed rather
        than hedged. The build-security baseline covers SHA-pinned actions, least-privilege tokens,
        a committed lockfile, Dependabot, an <code>npm audit</code> CI gate, and
        <code>security.txt</code>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={projectSchema} />
</DocLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    max-width: 65rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-16);
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .case-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .case-title {
    font-size: var(--text-4xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .case-subtitle {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .case-tech {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case-private {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .case-private svg {
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .case-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-10);
  }

  .case-section h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case-section h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .case-section p {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .feature-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding-left: 0;
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .feature-list li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .feature-list li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.6em;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    background-color: var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
  }

  .feature-list li strong {
    color: var(--color-text-heading);
  }

  .decision {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .decision h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .decision p {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
</style>
